    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fluid flow through fracture networks is an essential process in the accumulation of mineral and hydrocarbon resources. As fluid circulation also results in convective heat transfer, it is important for exploration models to consider the interaction of brittle deformation by fracturing, fluid flow and heat transfer when investigating temperature-dependent processes such as mineral precipitation and hydrocarbon maturation. Because the thermal history experienced by a given rock mass depends on the mechanisms of heat transfer and on the movement of the mass through static isotherms (uplift and burial), the study of this interaction in nature increasingly relies on precise radiometric dating techniques to determine the timing and rates of various inter-related processes.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
